"reasoning": "The patient presents with a burning pain in the stomach that comes and goes, accompanied by gas and bloating, loss of appetite, and weight loss. Most critically, the presence of dark and tarry stools (melena) and blood in the vomit (hematemesis) indicates upper gastrointestinal bleeding. These symptoms collectively are classic hallmarks of a bleeding peptic ulcer. While gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) can cause burning stomach pain and vomiting, it does not typically present with significant GI bleeding (melena and hematemesis) or profound weight loss. Typhoid typically presents with sustained fever and altered bowel habits rather than this specific constellation of chronic burning pain and overt upper GI bleeding. Therefore, the facts firmly establish peptic ulcer disease.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}

